From Apple docs on `isExcludedFromBackup` (Foundation): "Setting this property to true prevents the file or directory from being backed up... If you set it on a directory, the system does not back up the directory or any of its contents." I believe this is accurate for the modern backupd — BUT there are well-known reports (and Apple QA1719's structure) that files ADED LATER to an excluded directory ARE still excluded (backupd re-evaluates at backup time by walking; the directory flag prunes the walk). The main gotcha is with EXCLUDING files inside INCLUDED dirs. So setting on `.dap` dir alone is sufficient; walking descendants adds robustness for the upgrade path where... no wait — if the directory flag prunes the whole walk, descendants don't matter at all. The walker is belt-and-braces; harmless and cheap (dir is tiny). I'll include it — it directly matches the reviewer's "on the sandbox .dap subtree" wording.

