One important nuance: the flag should also be re-aplied to contents. Apple docs (QA1719): "If your application creates a folder... you can exclude the folder from backup by setting the attribute on the folder itself" — for folders, the backup exclusion applies to the folder; files created INSIDE an excluded folder are also excluded at backup time? Actually QA1719 says: "you can set this attribute on a directory to exclude the directory and its contents"... Let me be careful. The commonly cited doc: https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/qa/qa1719/_index.html "Technical Q&A QA1719 — How do I prevent files from being backed up to iCloud and iTunes?" It says: "Set the NSURLIsExcludedFromBackupKey on the file... For a directory, setting the attribute excludes the directory and all its contents" — I recall QA1719's option is about `NSURLIsExcludedFromBackupKey` for individual files AND there's a note that new files created in an excluded directory do NOT automatically inherit the flag?? Hmm.

Let me recall precisely. QA1719 lists:
1. Files that are restorable → Caches/ or tmp.
2. `NSURLIsExcludedFromBackupKey` — "iOS 5.1 and later ... the file will not be backed up"; "this attribute can be set on a directory to exclude it and its contents" — I'm fairly confident modern behavior: the backup daemon skips the entire subtree when the directory has the flag. There are many StackOverflow answers confirming directory-level exclusion covers contents (that's how apps exclude whole caches dirs).

But some sources note: the attribute must be applied to each file for app-level "do not back up"... The official doc for `isExcludedFromBackup`: "Setting this value to true on a directory excludes the directory and everything inside it from backups." Actually — Apple's documentation for URLResourceKey.isExcludedFromBackupKey states: "If the value is true, the resource is not backed up... For directories, all contents are also excluded." I'll go with directory-level exclusion on `.dap` — that's exactly what the review thread suggests ("set the NSURLIsExcludedFromBackupKey URL resource key on the sandbox .dap subtree").

To be extra safe (the reviewer says "subtree"), I can also walk existing children and set the flag on each — cheap at creation time (the dir is fresh/empty at that point, but a pre-existing install upgrading would have content). I'll implement: set on the directory + recursively on existing descendants. That covers the upgrade path where `.dap` already has keys/config from the previous app version.

